=== task: mcq medium
title: Why must they meet?
prompt: Inside the loop of a cyclic linked list, why can't fast jump over slow without ever landing on the same node?
* The distance from fast forward to slow shrinks by exactly 1 each step, so it must reach 0 :: Right. slow adds 1 to that distance and fast takes 2 away.
- fast moves at random :: Every move is fixed: two nodes per step.
- slow stops moving inside the loop :: Both keep moving; fast is only one node per step faster.
- The loop always has an even number of nodes :: Any loop length works.
hint: Measure how far fast is behind slow, going round the loop.
hint: How does that distance change in one step?
right: Each step changes the distance by +1 (slow) and −2 (fast): exactly −1. A distance that drops by 1 each time can't skip 0, so they land on the same node.
wrong: Going round the loop, fast is some number of nodes behind slow. Each step, that number goes down by exactly one, so it reaches 0, the meeting, within one lap.

